data.content:
The lightest approach is the **ambient `TimeProvider`** pattern (built into .NET 8+). No DI container needed—just accept a `TimeProvider` parameter with a default:

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _clock;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? clock = null)
        => _clock = clock ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _clock.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

In tests, use `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` (one NuGet package):

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

**Why this is the lightest option:**
- `TimeProvider` ships in the BCL (no new abstractions to maintain)
- The optional parameter means existing callers don't change
- `FakeTimeProvider` lets you advance time (`fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(5))`)

If you're targeting < .NET 8, the same idea works with a one-liner interface:

```csharp
public interface IClock { DateTimeOffset UtcNow { get; } }
```

